---
<Base
  title="Teaching Software Engineering"
  description="Teaching interests in programming, software engineering, and AI-assisted development, grounded in professional engineering experience."
>
  <section class="wrap pagehero">
    <div class="eyebrow">Teaching & education</div>
    <h1>Teaching Software Engineering</h1>
    <p class="lede">I approach software engineering education from the perspective of someone who has spent years building, maintaining, and modernizing software that organizations depend on. My goal is to connect technical fundamentals with the judgment, habits, and practical skills required to build dependable systems.</p>
    <p><a class="textlink" href="/hdse">Human-Directed Software Engineering</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight">
    <div class="bodycopy">
      <h2>How I Think About Teaching</h2>
      <p>These are the principles I want to bring into the classroom. They come from years of building and maintaining software, along with my own academic work.</p>

      <h3>Fundamentals provide independence</h3>
      <p>Students need enough understanding of programming languages, algorithms, data structures, systems, and software architecture to reason without depending entirely on frameworks or AI tools. Frameworks and assistants change. Conceptual understanding is what lets a graduate adapt when the stack under a project is replaced, or when a generated solution looks plausible but does not fit the problem. Independence is not hostility to tools; it is the ability to decide when a tool is wrong.</p>

      <h3>Learning happens through building</h3>
      <p>Programming is a practical discipline. Students should produce working software, encounter failure, debug problems, and revise their designs. Assignments should connect theory to observable behavior: a data structure that performs poorly under load, a schema that cannot express a required constraint, a test that catches a regression. The goal is competence that survives contact with incomplete requirements and imperfect tools—conditions that appear early in real projects and rarely disappear.</p>

      <h3>Explain and defend decisions</h3>
      <p>Working code alone is insufficient evidence of understanding. Students should be able to explain why they chose a design, what alternatives they considered, how they tested it, and where it might fail. That habit matters in industry review, and it matters even more when an assistant can produce a compiling solution that the author cannot account for. Assessment should make that explanation unavoidable.</p>

      <h3>Engineering is a human responsibility</h3>
      <p>Modern tools, including AI coding assistants, can accelerate implementation. Responsibility for correctness, maintainability, security, and the consequences of software remains with the engineers who build and release it. Instruction should treat that responsibility as part of the discipline, not as an optional ethics add-on at the end of the term.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight">
    <div class="sectionhead">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">Instructional interests</div>
        <h2>Areas of Instruction</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="muted" style="max-width:720px;margin:0 0 8px">Areas of professional and academic preparation for college-level instruction.</p>
    <div class="topicgrid">
      {topics.map((t) => (
        <article class="topic">
          <h3>{t.title}</h3>
          <ul>
            {t.items.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
          </ul>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight">
    <div class="bodycopy">
      <h2>Bringing Real Systems Into the Classroom</h2>
      <p>Much of what working engineers do is not greenfield invention. Long-lived production systems teach lessons that self-contained exercises rarely surface. The sample lessons below translate patterns from that kind of work into scoped instructional examples—without disclosing confidential client systems.</p>
      <p>Professional work has repeatedly required modernizing existing software rather than starting from scratch; working with complex business rules and changing requirements; designing databases that support operational workflows; integrating with legacy applications and third-party systems; diagnosing performance and reliability problems; and maintaining code written across different generations of technology.</p>
      <p class="pullquote">How do you change a system people already rely on without breaking what still works?</p>
      <p>Those experiences do not become course content by dumping proprietary details into a syllabus. They become content by translating patterns into understandable, appropriately scoped problems: a schema that must absorb a new workflow without breaking reports; an import path that must fail safely; an API whose contract has to survive a client that cannot be updated overnight. Students should leave with practice that resembles how software actually lives.</p>
      <p><a class="textlink" href="/work">Selected professional and independent work</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="spotlight">
    <div class="wrap">
    <div class="bodycopy">
      <h2>Teaching When AI Can Write Code</h2>
      <p>Coding assistants create a real challenge for traditional programming education: a student may produce a working solution without fully understanding how it works. Banning the tools ignores how software is now written. Celebrating them uncritically risks graduating people who cannot diagnose failures or evaluate tradeoffs.</p>
      <p>A better response is to change what we assess. Ask students to explain implementation decisions, reproduce and diagnose failures, write or critique tests and acceptance criteria, modify unfamiliar code, compare alternative implementations, and demonstrate conceptual understanding independent of the first draft an assistant produced. In that setting, speed of first compilation matters less than the quality of the student’s reasoning about the system.</p>
      <p>The objective is not to preserve the difficulty of typing code. It is to preserve—and strengthen—the intellectual work of engineering.</p>
      <p>That stance is the educational application of <a class="textlink" href="/hdse">Human-Directed Software Engineering (HDSE)</a>: humans direct the work, verification is independent of a model’s self-description, and durable project artifacts—not chat transcripts—hold the authoritative record.</p>
    </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight" id="sample-materials">
    <div class="sectionhead">
      <div>
        <div class="eyebrow">Sample instructional materials</div>
        <h2>Sample Teaching Materials</h2>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="bodycopy" style="margin-bottom:8px">
      <p>Sample lessons developed to demonstrate my approach to teaching programming and software engineering. They are written for community-college and undergraduate contexts and are complete materials you can read and adapt.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="materialrows">
      {lessons.map((lesson) => (
        <div class="materialrow">
          <div>
            <h3><a class="textlink" href={lesson.href}>{lesson.title}</a></h3>
            <p class="lesson-card-meta">{lesson.level} · {lesson.subject} · {lesson.duration}</p>
            <p>{lesson.description}</p>
          </div>
          <a class="textlink" href={lesson.href}>Open lesson</a>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="wrap section section-tight">
    <div class="bodycopy">
      <h2>Background</h2>
      <p>More than 15 years of professional software development, including designing, modernizing, and maintaining production applications across multiple industries. That work has also included preparing software documentation and training end users on systems I've built.</p>
      <div class="listcards" style="margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem">
        <div class="listcard">
          <div class="date">Completed · 2026</div>
          <h3>BS, Software Engineering</h3>
          <p>Western Governors University</p>
        </div>
        <div class="listcard">
          <div class="date">In progress</div>
          <h3>MS, Software Engineering — DevOps</h3>
          <p>Western Governors University · DevOps concentration · Accelerated BS-to-MS pathway</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>More context is on the <a class="textlink" href="/about">About</a> page. For conversations about courses, curriculum, or adjunct instruction, email <a class="textlink" href="mailto:colejd@gmail.com">colejd@gmail.com</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
